Topi Linkala <nes@iki.fi> wrote in message news:<cdhafp$8e1$1@nyytiset.pp.htv.fi>...

> This patch allows the insertion of Amulet of Yendor, Candelabrum of 
> Invocation, Bell of Opening and Book of Dead into a container. It also 
> prevents naming these items.

This is a very cool idea I think, all the more so because it must have
been rather tricky to implement.  You've caught all the situations I
can think of that would prevent monsters from stealing the items or
allowing the player to escape the effects of the Amulet of Yendor,
while making sure the game doesn't become unwinnable.

A couple of other adjustments I can think of, take 'em or leave 'em:
1. The point bonus for winning with the Bell of Opening, Book of the
Dead, and/or the Candelabrum of Invocation probably takes into account
the player having to carry them outside of a Bag of Holding, with
their weight slowing him down.

2. I'm not sure that making it difficult for the player to keep track
of the real Amulet is such a good idea.  Attaching a penalty for
naming the Amulet strikes me as punishing cleverness for no reason
other than it'd break the game if it worked.  There are other ways to
identify fake amulets of course (Identify, try level 'porting with the
amulet, fakes are destructable & (I think) are made of a different
material), if making the player resort to those is your goal then it
may be a positive addition.

3. If I remember correctly, isn't the result of a Bag of Holding
explosion, when the bag contains the Amulet, that it's teleported to a
random location in the entire dungeon?  If that's true, then there's a
rather great potential for abuse if the player has a spare bag: since
you get the Amulet on the bottom level of the whole dungeon, if the
player were to purposely explode a bag then, anywhere the Amulet could
end up would be better than where it is at that point.  Then it simply
becomes a matter of finding it again, but that's still easier than
dragging it back up the stairs.  (If I'm remembering wrong then please
correct me.)

